# wa-codex-broker-admin sudoers drop-in — passwordless root for the WA codex
# broker's dedicated-binary admin verbs (status/bump). Installed as
# /etc/sudoers.d/wa-codex-broker-admin, 0440 root:wheel, by
# scripts/install_wa_codex_admin.sh, which `visudo -cf`'s a temp copy of
# THIS file before it ever lands in /etc/sudoers.d — a syntax error here
# must fail that install, never land half-written on the live sudoers tree.
#
# Fixes the "pin drift" outage class (W136): before this, an operator who
# hit a version-pin pause needed an INTERACTIVE sudo password on Pro to cure
# it (see the two-line `sed` + `launchctl kickstart` cure the seat sentinel
# used to print). After this, the `nuzantara` interactive user can run the
# cure directly.
#
# ROUND 1 (after PR #7313's security BLOCK): round 0 shipped an env-var
# `WA_CODEX_ADMIN_TEST_MODE` escape hatch INSIDE the installed script, and
# this drop-in did not stop the invoking user's environment from reaching
# it — sudo's default `env_reset` clears most variables, but nothing here
# said so explicitly, and nothing forbade `-E`/`VAR=val sudo ...` for THIS
# command specifically. Round 1 removed that escape hatch from the script
# entirely (it no longer exists at all — see D8 in the round-1 spec), and
# this drop-in now ALSO closes the policy layer belt-and-suspenders: a
# command-specific `env_reset` Default plus `NOSETENV` on the rule itself
# means no environment variable the invoking user sets can reach this
# command's execution, regardless of `-E` or a leading `VAR=val`.
Defaults!/usr/local/libexec/wa-codex-broker-admin.sh env_reset

# IMPORTANT — the `bump *` wildcard below is NOT the security boundary.
# sudoers command-line-argument wildcards are matched via glob(3)/fnmatch(3)
# (man sudoers, "Wildcards" section): `*` matches ANY set of zero or more
# characters INCLUDING WHITE SPACE. That means this line, on its own, would
# equally match `bump 1.2.3; rm -rf /` or `bump ../../etc/passwd` as ONE
# argument string — sudoers has no concept of "one safe token" here, only a
# glob over the whole trailing string. The real guard lives INSIDE
# /usr/local/libexec/wa-codex-broker-admin.sh itself: a strict
# `^[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$` regex on the version argument, a hard reject of
# any extra argument, and `set -euo pipefail` with no `eval` and no argument
# ever built into a shell string. This drop-in only decides WHO may invoke
# the two verbs as root, with what environment — never whether a given
# argument is safe to run.
nuzantara ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: NOSETENV: /usr/local/libexec/wa-codex-broker-admin.sh status, /usr/local/libexec/wa-codex-broker-admin.sh bump *
